# /etc/crawler/render-worker@9109.env — the per-instance override of the
# second render-worker instance (crawler-render-worker@9109.service; the
# template reads it AFTER the shared /etc/crawler/crawler.env —
# same-named variables win, docs/deploy.md §2.2). The chromium PIN of
# the moli primary rollout (T-337, docs/deploy.md §2.4): when the shared
# environment moves worker A to the moli engine
# (CRAWLER_RENDER_ENGINE=moli + CRAWLER_RENDER_REMOTE_URL=ws://127.0.0.1:9224),
# this file keeps the @9109 instance on the shared chromium unit (:9222).
# Without the pin it would silently follow the shared environment to
# moli and double the load on ONE engine process (the single moli
# process serves all its contexts); the pinned chromium share is also
# the live per-engine comparison base in
# crawler_render_records_total{engine}.
#
# Only the engine selection is pinned: the other render knobs (contexts,
# timeouts, restart_every_pages) keep coming from the shared
# environment — the SAME values for both engines is the honest
# comparison. CRAWLER_RENDER_RESTART_MEMORY_BYTES=0 of the shared
# rollout block lands here too and is inert: at an external chromium the
# memory threshold never worked (the config validation tolerates it —
# the cap job belongs to crawler-chromium.service's MemoryMax).
#
# Installation (docs/deploy.md §2.4, BEFORE the shared switch;
# permissions as for crawler.env, §4.5):
#   install -o root -g crawler -m 0640 /dev/stdin /etc/crawler/render-worker@9109.env \
#     < deploy/systemd/render-worker@9109.env.example
#   systemctl restart crawler-render-worker@9109

CRAWLER_RENDER_ENGINE=chromium
CRAWLER_RENDER_REMOTE_URL=ws://127.0.0.1:9222
